@startuml object-lifecycle
title The lifecycle of a coroutine — design/execution.md

skinparam defaultTextAlignment left
skinparam ArrowColor #444444

state "create" as Create
state "queued" as Queued
state "released" as Released

note top of Create
  **Only the four boxes below are values of the state word.**
  `create`, `queued` and `released` are stages of a lifetime and
  are not states: there are three parking states, and `Terminal`
  is a fourth value of the word that is not a parking state.
end note

[*] --> Create : allocate the object; take a stack\nfor a stackful coroutine

Create --> Queued : enqueue. A third enqueue, outside the\nwake invariant: the coroutine has no record\nyet and no waker can name it

Queued --> Running : the scheduler mounts it. Dispatch reads\nthe suspension kind here, once, and\nbranches to one of two resume routines

Running --> Parked : the **worker** stores it once the suspension\nhas finished — park step 4, unmount hook\nreason **Park**

Running --> Woken : park step 4 with the cancelled bit set: the\n**worker** stores Woken and enqueues, and\nthe coroutine is never Parked

Parked --> Woken : whoever ends the wait, and the same caller\nenqueues. One enqueue per wake, because\none caller moves Parked to Woken and a\nsecond finds Woken already set and returns

Woken --> Running : the scheduler mounts and resumes. For a\ndeclaring actor in WokenShared the mount is\na compare-exchange to Running(W), and\nwhoever loses does not mount

Running --> Terminal : the worker, once the coroutine's own code has\nfinished — unmount hook reason **Boundary**

Terminal --> Released : the last reference goes

Released --> [*]

note right of Parked
  **Woken means the wait is decided.**
  Whoever moves Parked to Woken has already
  claimed the winner or taken remaining to zero,
  so a later signal for the same wait finds
  nothing to do.
end note

note right of Terminal
  The order inside teardown: the unmount hook with
  reason Boundary, the Terminal store, the join
  wakes, the release of the wait record, the return
  of the stack, the drop of the scheduler's reference.

  Storing Terminal earlier would report an ordinary
  unwind as a deadlock, because a terminated holder
  is one that never releases. Storing it later would
  let a retired completion pass wake step 0 and read
  the epoch out of a record already freed.

  The join wakes follow the store, which is what lets
  the detector read a terminal target as live: a pass
  that sees Terminal knows the wake is on its way.
end note

note bottom
  **An actor that declares mid-message movement carries a wider word**,
  and only such an actor does: Running(W), Parked(W), WokenLocal(W),
  WokenShared, Terminal — the owning worker beside the state, atomic
  stores instead of plain ones, and the cancelled bit moved to a byte
  beside it. For every other coroutine "its own thread" is the thread it has
  always run on, and no wake of it is ever dispatched to another worker.
end note

@enduml
